^w©5ÖK:iò¸&jkU{"url":"/gcsl/language/lists-and-control-flow/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/lists-and-control-flow/","content":"Lists and Control Flow. Lists hold ordered values of one element type. GCSL's loops are expressions, so a loop can collect results into a list or run only for its effects. This avoids a separate collection-building language. Lists and ranges # List<Int> and Int[] name the same type. Indexes start at zero; a negative or out-of-range index is a runtime error. A spread inserts another list's elements. An empty list needs an expected element type: Copylet empty: List<Int> = [] let values = [...empty, 3, 8, 5] values[1] 0..3 produces [0, 1, 2]; 0..=3 includes 3. Bounds are integers and ranges run upward. A range used directly as a loop's iterable is counted without first allocating the list of all its integers. Collecting and repeating # A loop used as a value collects each iteration's result. A guard skips iterations that don't match: Copylet doubled = for n in [1, 2, 3] if n > 1 { n * 2 } doubled A loop used as a statement discards its results. Use for condition for repetition while a condition holds, for { ... } without a condition, or do { ... } for condition to test after the body. A header can contain initialization and an update: Copylet values = for let i = 0; i < 3; i := i + 1 { i * 2 } values A plain header variable can be rebound by the header. Write let mut if the body must also update it. Leaving a computation # break ends the enclosing loop and continue skips to its next iteration. Either can supply a value to the collecting loop before leaving that iteration. An unlabeled return returns from the enclosing function or lazy body. Labels identify an outer loop or a block. A labeled return leaves that target with one value; it does not append to a loop's accumulated list. Control flow cannot jump out through a function boundary. See gcsl(5) for label syntax and the distinction between collected and returned values.","word_count":337,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Lists and Control Flow"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"lists-and-ranges","text":"Lists and ranges #","location":37},{"element":"h2","id":"collecting-and-repeating","text":"Collecting and repeating #","location":122},{"element":"h2","id":"leaving-a-computation","text":"Leaving a computation #","location":249}]}},"word_data":{"bound":[{"position":96,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"onli":[{"position":27,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"alloc":[{"position":115,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"header":[{"position":200,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":231,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":238,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"of":[{"position":8,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":55,"weight":8,"original_word":null},{"position":118,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"through":[{"position":320,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"rang":[{"position":39,"weight":144,"original_word":null},{"position":55,"weight":8,"original_word":null},{"position":100,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":104,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"initi":[{"position":203,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"continu":[{"position":259,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"return":[{"position":280,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":281,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":299,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":335,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"run":[{"position":26,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":101,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"integ":[{"position":98,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":121,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"type":[{"position":11,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":47,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":74,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"index":[{"position":48,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":56,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"doe":[{"p